  abstract = {Childhood is a critical period for the emergence of mental health issues (McGrath et al., 2023). Many children already experience such problems (Kaman et al., 2025; WHO, 2025) and face long waiting times to receive support (Eichstedt et al., 2024). In the initial therapeutic encounter, relationship building is important, and children are more likely to engage with playful and age-appropriate communication styles (Bhide & Chakraborty, 2020). This approach might be supported by social robots, whose appearance is appealing and evokes especially positive feelings (Anastasiou et al., 2019) and children tend to respond to them (Vollmer et al., 2018). One example is the QTrobot, which is considered well-suited for studying child-robot interaction, as its human-like features and physical presence foster socio-emotional engagement (Zibetti et al., 2025). Robots are increasingly used to enhance treatment motivation, communication and emotional support (Mutluer et al., 2026). For instance, in children with autism (Bettencourt et al., 2025), in clinical (Or et al., 2025) and educational contexts (Belpaeme et al., 2018). Tablets are another prominent technology in children’s lives (Hardman & Lilley, 2023) and can enrich interactions (Studhalter et al., 2024), support development (De Souza et al., 2025) and learning, when used appropriately (Hardman & Lilley, 2023). Overall, social interaction is a natural tendency in children (Seehagen et al., 2025). In those, humans remain a central comp},
}

